version: 2
title: How a version-1 calendar works
description: A lens over one kanban board — the schedule its edges, durations and due date imply, laid on a month grid on every open; nothing is stored here.
events:
  - key: always
    label: Always
    trigger: imposed
    domain: Always
    detail: What holds at every moment — the shape of the file, what it does not hold, and where the engine lives.
    content:
      key: shape
      label: The shape of the file
      kind: brief
      doc:
        title: The shape of the file
        description: Four keys; the dates live nowhere.
        sections:
          - title: Top-level keys
            body: |
              | key | what it is |
              |---|---|
              | `title` | the heading |
              | `description` | one line under it; absent, the view says "Derived schedule — dependency edges chained backward from the due date." |
              | `board` | the `.kanban` this calendar derives from, resolved against this file's folder |
              | `due` | optional — overrides the board's own `due` as the anchor |

              A bare `2026-09-09` is read by YAML as a date and kept as the day written; a string
              that parses as a date is cut to its first ten characters; anything else is dropped.
          - title: What it does not hold
            body: |
              No dates of its own. Like a `.tablediff`, the calendar recomputes from its source on
              every open, so it can never be a copy that drifts from the board. Everything drawn is
              the board's `needs`, `duration` and `due`.
          - title: Where the engine lives
            body: |
              `packages/filekinds/src/lib/calendarDoc.ts` — `parseCalendarLens`; its header comment
              is what `studio-check --spec calendar` prints. The schedule is `kanbanDoc.ts`'s
              `scheduleTasks` and the grid is `journeyStages.ts`'s `monthGrid`, so both calendars in
              the suite read the same way. The view is `components/kanban/CalendarLensView.tsx`,
              which opens the kanban's `TaskDialog.tsx`; the checker is `apps/cli/src/check.ts`.
  - key: opened
    label: The file is opened
    trigger: imposed
    domain: Opening
    detail: The lens is parsed, the board is read and resolved, the schedule is computed backward from the due date and laid on the grid.
    content:
      key: derive
      label: What is derived, and what is drawn
      kind: brief
      doc:
        title: What is derived, and what is drawn
        description: Four steps between the file and the grid.
        sections:
          - title: Reading the board
            body: |
              `parseCalendarLens` never throws (an unparseable file has no title and no board).
              The `board` ref is resolved against this file's folder and read; the board is parsed
              by `parseKanban` and its `source:` resolved the same way the board view does, so the
              calendar schedules the same merged tasks the board shows. No ref: "No `board:` ref —
              nothing to derive from." An unreadable ref: "could not read <path>".
          - title: The schedule
            body: |
              `scheduleTasks(board, lens.due)` — as late as possible. Walking the dependency layers
              from the deepest up, each task ends at the earliest of its own `due`, the board's
              `due` (the lens's `due` in its place when given), and the day before its earliest
              dependent starts; it starts `duration − 1` days before that (duration at least 1,
              rounded). A task with no anchor at all, or in a cycle, is left unscheduled. The
              scheduled tasks come back sorted by start, then title.
          - title: The grid
            body: |
              `monthGrid` picks the month: the due date; without one, the median start of the
              scheduled tasks; without tasks, today. Weeks run Monday to Sunday from the Monday on
              or before the 1st until the month ends, each cell carrying the tasks whose start and
              end enclose the day. The grid's title is the month and year.
          - title: What is drawn
            body: |
              The title, the description (or the default line) and "Due <date>." A weekday header
              and the cells: days outside the month dimmed, the due day with an amber ring, today
              with a darker border and "· today". Each task is a chip in every day it covers,
              coloured by its status column's colour (grey when the column has none), titled
              "<task> · <start> → <end>". Below, when any: "Unscheduled (no due anywhere downstream,
              or a dependency cycle): a · b". The grid needs 720px and scrolls sideways under that.
  - key: chip-clicked
    label: A task chip is clicked
    trigger: chosen
    arity: many
    domain: Reading
    detail: The board's own task dialog opens on that task; the calendar edits nothing.
    content:
      key: dialog
      label: What opens
      kind: md
      doc: |
        The same `TaskDialog` the board opens, on the board's document with the board's folder as
        base: the note, the "needs" and "depended on by" chips (a click jumps to that task), the
        body as markdown, and the task's file rendered by its kind — a `.brief` sliced to the
        task's `section`. No pencil, no trash: the calendar never writes, and neither does the
        dialog it borrows. A chip is found by the task's title, so two tasks with one title open
        the first.
  - key: changed-on-disk
    label: The file changes on disk
    trigger: imposed
    arity: many
    domain: Reading
    detail: The lens re-parses; the board is read again only when its path changed.
    content:
      key: reread
      label: What is re-read
      kind: md
      doc: |
        The desktop watcher (250 ms debounce, `studio:fs-changed`), the web folder entry's server
        events or VS Code's text document deliver the new text, and the Studio re-reads unless its
        autosave is dirty, saving or in error. The lens re-parses: a new `due` re-schedules at
        once; a new `board` ref reads the new board. The board file itself is read when the
        resolved path changes and on open — a board edited while its calendar sits open shows on
        the calendar's next open. The open dialog stays on its key.
  - key: grid-elsewhere
    label: A journey draws the same grid
    trigger: imposed
    domain: Elsewhere
    detail: The month grid is shared; the calendar file is one of two things that fill it.
    content:
      key: elsewhere
      label: The other calendar in the suite
      kind: md
      doc: |
        A staged journey's stage with `calendar:` lays its task lists' `start` and `end` fields
        on the same `monthGrid`, coloured by group and legended by source list. That grid reads
        lists, not a board, and lives in the `.definition` kind; this file is the lens over one
        `.kanban`. The two look alike on purpose.
  - key: checked
    label: studio-check runs on it
    trigger: imposed
    domain: Writing
    detail: One file in, one verdict out — the board is not read.
    content:
      key: checker
      label: What the checker does
      kind: brief
      doc:
        title: What the checker does
        description: The order it runs in, and what it leaves alone.
        sections:
          - title: In order
            body: |
              1. Parse the YAML — a parse error is the only problem reported.
              2. `parseCalendarLens` — it never throws. No summary line: `ok <name>  Calendar`.
          - title: Not checked
            body: |
              That `board` is present, exists, or is a kanban — the view says "No `board:` ref" or
              "could not read". That `due` was a date (a bad one is dropped, silently). Neither the
              command line nor the page reads the board.
  - key: authored
    label: A calendar is written
    trigger: chosen
    domain: Writing
    content:
      key: write
      label: Writing one
      kind: guide
      doc:
        title: Write a calendar
        description: Three lines beside a board that already has edges and a due date.
        steps:
          - key: board
            label: Give the board what a schedule needs
            detail: |
              In the `.kanban`: `due` on the board, `needs` between tasks, `duration` where a task
              takes more than a day. Without a due date anywhere, every task is unscheduled.
          - key: template
            label: Start from the template
            after: [board]
            detail: |
              `studio-check --template calendar > new.calendar` in the board's folder:

              ```yaml
              title: "new"
              description: "A lens over one board: …"
              board: board.kanban   # a ref resolved against this file's folder
              # due: 2026-12-31     # optional — overrides the board's own due date
              ```

              Point `board` at the file. Uncomment `due` only to plan against another date than
              the board's.
          - key: check
            label: Check it, then open it
            after: [template]
            detail: |
              `studio-check new.calendar` — only YAML can fail. Open it: a task listed under
              "Unscheduled" has no due date anywhere downstream, or sits in a cycle.
